    <!-- Add Activity -->

 <activity>
     <title>Discovering the Cosine Rule Using String and Cardboard</title>
     <statement>
        <p>
            Work in groups of 3–4 to physically investigate the relationship between the sides and an included angle of a triangle. Through hands-on construction and measurement, you will discover and derive the Cosine Rule.
        </p>

        <p>
            <alert>Learning Objective</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            Investigate the relationship between the sides and an included angle of a triangle and derive the Cosine Rule through direct measurement and algebraic reasoning.
        </p>

        <p>
            <alert>Materials Needed</alert>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><p>Manila paper or cardboard</p></li>
            <li><p>String (various lengths)</p></li>
            <li><p>Ruler (at least 30 cm)</p></li>
            <li><p>Protractor</p></li>
            <li><p>Scissors</p></li>
            <li><p>Marker pens</p></li>
            <li><p>Calculator</p></li>
            <li><p>Push pins or thumbtacks (for extension activity)</p></li>
        </ul>

        <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Step 1: Construct a Triangle.</alert> In your group:
                </p>
                <ol marker="(i)">
                    <li><p>Draw a large triangle ABC on the cardboard.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Label the sides as follows:
                        <ul>
                            <li><m>AB = c</m></li>
                            <li><m>BC = a</m></li>
                            <li><m>AC = b</m></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p></li>
                    <li><p>Using a ruler, measure all three side lengths as accurately as possible.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Using a protractor, measure angle <m>C</m> (the angle at vertex <m>C</m> between sides <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>).</p></li>
                    <li><p>Record all measurements in the table below.</p></li>
                </ol>
                <table xml:id="triangle-measurements-table">
                    <title>Original Triangle Measurements</title>
                    <tabular halign="center">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                            <cell>Measurement</cell>
                            <cell>Value (cm or degrees)</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Side <m>a</m> (BC)</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Side <m>b</m> (AC)</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Side <m>c</m> (AB)</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Angle <m>C</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <note component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        Encourage students to measure multiple times and take an average for accuracy. The triangle should be drawn large (at least 15 cm on each side) to minimize measurement errors.
                    </p>
                </note>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Step 2: Create a Right Triangle by Drawing a Perpendicular.</alert>
                </p>
                <ol marker="(i)">
                    <li><p>From vertex <m>A</m>, draw a perpendicular line to side <m>BC</m> (or the line containing <m>BC</m>).</p></li>
                    <li><p>Label the foot of the perpendicular as point <m>D</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Observe that the original triangle has now been divided into two right-angled triangles: <m>\triangle ACD</m> and <m>\triangle ABD</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
                <note component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        Students may notice that if angle <m>C</m> is obtuse, point <m>D</m> will fall outside the original triangle (on the extension of side <m>BC</m>). This is fine and should be discussed as a valid case.
                    </p>
                </note>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Step 3: Measure the Components of the Right Triangle.</alert>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Carefully measure the following distances on your cardboard diagram:
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><p><m>AD</m> (the perpendicular height)</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>DC</m> (the horizontal distance from <m>D</m> to <m>C</m>)</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>BD</m> (the horizontal distance from <m>B</m> to <m>D</m>)</p></li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                    Record these measurements below:
                </p>
                <table xml:id="perpendicular-measurements-table">
                    <title>Perpendicular Components</title>
                    <tabular halign="center">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                            <cell>Measurement</cell>
                            <cell>Measured Value (cm)</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Height <m>AD</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Distance <m>DC</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Distance <m>BD</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p>
                    Now, using angle <m>C</m> from Step 1, calculate what <m>DC</m> and <m>AD</m> should be theoretically:
                </p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>DC = b\cos C</mrow>
                    <mrow>AD = b\sin C</mrow>
                </md>
                <p>
                    <strong>Show your calculations:</strong>
                </p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>DC = b\cos C = \underline{\hspace{3cm}} \times \cos(\underline{\hspace{1cm}}) = \underline{\hspace{2cm}}\text{ cm}</mrow>
                    <mrow>AD = b\sin C = \underline{\hspace{3cm}} \times \sin(\underline{\hspace{1cm}}) = \underline{\hspace{2cm}}\text{ cm}</mrow>
                </md>
                <table xml:id="calculated-components-table">
                    <title>Calculated vs. Measured Components</title>
                    <tabular halign="center">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                            <cell>Component</cell>
                            <cell>Measured (cm)</cell>
                            <cell>Calculated (cm)</cell>
                            <cell>Difference</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>DC</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>AD</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <note component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        This step connects trigonometry to the geometry. Small differences between measured and calculated values are expected and should be discussed as measurement error.
                    </p>
                </note>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Step 4: Apply the Pythagorean Theorem to Right Triangle ABD.</alert>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Focus on the right triangle <m>\triangle ABD</m>, where the right angle is at <m>D</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Using the Pythagorean theorem:
                </p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>c^2 = AD^2 + BD^2</mrow>
                </md>
                <p>
                    Notice that:
                </p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>BD = a - DC = a - b\cos C</mrow>
                </md>
                <p>
                    Substitute the expressions for <m>AD</m> and <m>BD</m>:
                </p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>c^2 = (b\sin C)^2 + (a - b\cos C)^2</mrow>
                </md>
                <p>
                    <strong>Calculate this for your triangle:</strong>
                </p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>c^2 = (\underline{\hspace{2cm}})^2 + (\underline{\hspace{2cm}} - \underline{\hspace{2cm}})^2</mrow>
                    <mrow>c^2 = \underline{\hspace{2cm}} + \underline{\hspace{2cm}}</mrow>
                    <mrow>c^2 = \underline{\hspace{2cm}}</mrow>
                    <mrow>c = \underline{\hspace{2cm}}\text{ cm}</mrow>
                </md>
                <p>
                    Compare this calculated value of <m>c</m> with the measured value from Step 1. How close are they?
                </p>
                <note component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        Students should see that their calculated value is very close to their measured value from Step 1, validating the approach.
                    </p>
                </note>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Step 5: Expand and Simplify to Derive the Cosine Rule.</alert>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Starting from:
                </p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>c^2 = (b\sin C)^2 + (a - b\cos C)^2</mrow>
                </md>
                <p>
                    Expand the squared terms:
                </p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>c^2 = b^2\sin^2 C + a^2 - 2ab\cos C + b^2\cos^2 C</mrow>
                </md>
                <p>
                    Group the <m>b^2</m> terms:
                </p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>c^2 = a^2 + b^2(\sin^2 C + \cos^2 C) - 2ab\cos C</mrow>
                </md>
                <p>
                    Use the fundamental trigonometric identity <m>\sin^2 C + \cos^2 C = 1</m>:
                </p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>c^2 = a^2 + b^2(1) - 2ab\cos C</mrow>
                </md>
                <p>
                    Simplify:
                </p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>c^2 = a^2 + b^2 - 2ab\cos C</mrow>
                </md>
                <p>
                    <strong>This is the Cosine Rule!</strong>
                </p>
                <note component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        Work through this expansion carefully with students. Emphasize how the Pythagorean theorem and trigonometric substitutions lead naturally to the cosine rule. Highlight the trigonometric identity as a key step.
                    </p>
                </note>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Step 6: Discussion and Discovery.</alert> Answer the following questions in your group:
                </p>
                <ol marker="(i)">
                    <li><p>How is the Cosine Rule related to Pythagoras' Theorem? What is the connection?</p></li>
                    <li><p>What happens to the cosine rule when <m>C = 90°</m>?</p></li>
                    <li><p>Why does the term <m>-2ab\cos C</m> disappear when <m>C = 90°</m>?</p></li>
                    <li><p>If angle <m>C</m> is very small (close to <m>0°</m>), what would happen to the length of side <m>c</m>? Explain using the cosine rule.</p></li>
                    <li><p>If angle <m>C</m> is very large (close to <m>180°</m>), what would happen to the length of side <m>c</m>? Explain using the cosine rule.</p></li>
                </ol>
                <solution component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        Expected responses:
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p><strong>(i)</strong> The Cosine Rule is a generalization of Pythagoras' Theorem. When <m>C = 90°</m>, the cosine rule reduces to the Pythagorean theorem because <m>\cos(90°) = 0</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p><strong>(ii)</strong> When <m>C = 90°</m>: <m>c^2 = a^2 + b^2 - 2ab(0) = a^2 + b^2</m>, which is Pythagoras' Theorem.</p></li>
                        <li><p><strong>(iii)</strong> When <m>C = 90°</m>, <m>\cos(90°) = 0</m>, so the term <m>-2ab\cos C = -2ab(0) = 0</m> and disappears.</p></li>
                        <li><p><strong>(iv)</strong> As <m>C</m> approaches <m>0°</m>, <m>\cos C</m> approaches 1, so <m>-2ab\cos C</m> becomes very negative, making <m>c</m> very small. The triangle becomes flat.</p></li>
                        <li><p><strong>(v)</strong> As <m>C</m> approaches <m>180°</m>, <m>\cos C</m> approaches <m>-1</m>, so <m>-2ab\cos C</m> becomes very positive, making <m>c</m> large. The triangle becomes nearly flat again but "open" the other way.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </solution>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Extension Activity: Verify the Cosine Rule with Variable Angles.</alert>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Using string and push pins on a notice board:
                </p>
                <ol marker="(i)">
                    <li><p>Fix sides <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> at their original lengths using string.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Keep point <m>C</m> and one endpoint of side <m>a</m> fixed.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Vary angle <m>C</m> by rotating side <m>b</m> to different angles: <m>45°</m>, <m>60°</m>, <m>90°</m>, <m>120°</m>, <m>135°</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>For each angle, measure the resulting length of side <m>c</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Use the cosine rule to predict what <m>c</m> should be for each angle.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Complete the verification table below.</p></li>
                </ol>
                <table xml:id="extension-verification-table">
                    <title>Extension: Verification of Cosine Rule with Variable Angles</title>
                    <tabular halign="center">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                            <cell>Angle <m>C</m> (degrees)</cell>
                            <cell>Measured <m>c</m> (cm)</cell>
                            <cell>Formula Prediction (cm)</cell>
                            <cell>Difference</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>45°</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>60°</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>90°</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>120°</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>135°</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p>
                    What patterns do you observe? Does the cosine rule accurately predict the length of side <m>c</m> for all angles?
                </p>
                <note component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        This extension makes the effect of the <m>\cos C</m> term physically visible. Students will see that as the angle changes, the length of side <m>c</m> changes predictably according to the cosine rule. This demonstrates that the formula is universal and powerful.
                    </p>
                </note>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Group Presentation and Conclusion.</alert> 
                </p>
                <p>
                    Prepare a brief presentation for the class that includes:
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><p>Your original triangle with all measurements labeled</p></li>
                    <li><p>The perpendicular construction and measurements</p></li>
                    <li><p>The step-by-step derivation of the cosine rule</p></li>
                    <li><p>A summary of your discovery: How does the cosine rule generalize Pythagoras' Theorem?</p></li>
                    <li><p>Your findings from the extension activity (if completed)</p></li>
                </ul>
                <note component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        Encourage students to explain their reasoning clearly and use their cardboard diagrams and measurements as visual aids. This develops mathematical communication skills and reinforces conceptual understanding.
                    </p>
                </note>
            </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </activity>
    

